From: DeltaVector1 <DeltaVector1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 13:08:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 31/31] Double the rollout tunnels to 48; add the in-flight
 sweep harness

max_inflight_rollouts is not the lever. Measured two windows at each value,
because run-to-run spread is about +/-13% and a single window cannot separate
settings that differ by less:

  128 in flight: 15.70 and 12.20 -> mean 13.95/min, 0.17 timeouts/min
  192 in flight: 16.75 and 11.44 -> mean 14.10/min, 7.7  timeouts/min

Same accepted rate, roughly 45x the timeouts. The link saturates near 14
accepted rollouts a minute and everything above that becomes wasted work. The
engines agree: they run 3 to 7 concurrent against a max_num_seqs of 128.

So add flows instead. Ports 18025-18048, even on h200a and odd on h200b to keep
the parity convention the health ports derive from. Aggregate throughput over a
lossy path scales with the number of TCP flows, and all requests to one port
multiplex as channels over a single TCP connection, where one lost packet
stalls every channel behind it. Going 8 -> 24 tunnels previously took
collection from about 4 completions a minute to 13-16.

tunnel_watchdog.sh probes 18001-18048 now; ports it does not probe never get
repaired.

scripts/ops/sweep_inflight.sh measures one value per run. It warms up until
accepted >= 64 rather than for a fixed time: a restarted orchestrator starts at
zero, rollouts take minutes, and acceptance is group-granular, so a fixed 180s
warmup opened the window at accepted=0 and read 4.57/min where the steady rate
was 14. SKIP_RESTART=1 measures the running value without paying a restart.

